7592186134976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1045 "The Intermediate Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "66b245e8-8d46-5eb6-a9cb-721c5323145e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1061 24962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e944fcc1-fe2f-5c5d-9768-0e36d6d884b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1061 564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8f1b0d3-494f-537b-8e99-af2aca4fc4f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1061 565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce1aa6a3-c0f2-525f-a909-c241964d206f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1061 568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "804af12a-0f60-55f9-a36a-0d40e8fd270b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1061 570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec27384a-d1f9-523f-b3ef-d8bed946e5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1061 571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79d4ad5d-f388-5fc5-a8b7-a72f14369138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1064 "The **intermediate value theorem (IVT)** states that:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is continuous on $\\color{blue}[a,b],$ and $k$ is a number between $\\color{red}{f(a)}$ and $\\color{red}{f(b)}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in \\color{blue}{[a,b]}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nThe intuition behind the IVT is that if a function is continuous between two inputs, then between those two inputs, we must be able to draw the function without picking up our pen off the page, so the function must cover all values between the two corresponding outputs.\n\nThe IVT is often used to prove that some equation has a solution. For example, if we want to prove that there is a solution to $f(x)=3,$ and we know that $f(x)$ is a continuous function with $f({\\color{blue}{1}})={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $f({\\color{blue}{6}})={\\color{red}{6}},$ then the IVT tells us that there exists some solution $c \\in \\color{blue}{[1,6]}$ such that $f(c)=3.$\n\nWe can gain further intuition by visualizing the situation. First, let's plot what $y=f(x)$ *might* look like.\n\n![](images/98/9896ea1c3d3d7c461e78ce4b4b232ce1f13f329468cd28b2d8e743132de093df.png)\n\nFor this particular $f(x)$ there is one solution to $f(x)=3$, which is $x\\approx 2.$ Note that our solution lies inside the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\nNow let's consider another *possible* $f(x).$\n\n![](images/de/dea5683d5b18bccf9623fb6a107e8ef818ddd1cef4adbef08be471869791ec27.png)\n\nThis time, there are three solutions to $f(x)=3$ which lie in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$ \n\nWe can keep changing our $f(x),$ but no matter how we draw it, we will always have some solution to $f(x)=3$ which lies in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\n![](images/4d/4d4aaec84baff10a48f715eeae7e06af21beccd29b38c42b3283032ef0726fec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1065 306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9dc98e46-26fd-5ae1-b12b-05da48928cc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+5, & 1\\le x<3 \\\\ -x+4, & 3\\le x<4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1065 309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6571cd4-d390-5e75-907d-726b500e6976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1067 "Why Does the Function Have to Be Continuous?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a513028-7494-52ef-ad77-c476ad1110db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f653602-ed65-5294-8d6c-a6ef40f95a14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f729d70-c219-5012-b6a4-33000a396e60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5ab31d1-2bed-5320-91a1-aa82ac71d5a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "01a7db5c-f3b0-55a6-bffd-8b68714c8c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae2fb37c-3d07-5853-b99d-9e361dcd461b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
